            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": ": Spell-like ability at caster level 4th or higher."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "node_type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": "Benefit",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": ": Choose one of your spell-like abilities, subject to the restrictions described in this feat. You can use the chosen spell-like ability as a scarring spell-like ability three times per day (or less, if the ability is normally usable only once or twice per day)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "node_type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": "When you use a scarring spell-like ability, a target that fails its saving throw against the spell-like ability takes a –2 penalty on saving throws against emotion and fear effects you create, and a –1 penalty on saving throws against other emotion and fear effects, for the next 24 hours. If the spelllike ability doesn’t allow a saving throw, you can’t choose to enhance it with this feat. Penalties from multiple scarring spell-like abilities don’t stack. This feat can be applied only to spell-like abilities with the emotion"
            },
            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": "UM",
              "marks": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": " or fear descriptor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "node_type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "node_type": "text",
              "value": "You can only select a spell-like ability duplicating a spell with a level less than or equal to 1/2 your caster level (round down) – 1. For a summary, see Table 3–1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "node_type": "paragraph",
